<h2>The transaction and its collapse</h2>
<p>On 15 February 2016, the parties signed an Agreement to Sell for property at C-490, Yojna Vihar, Delhi — 336.87 square yards at Rs 12.8 crore. The buyer paid Rs 1.5 crore at signing, described in the document as bayana, with the balance due by 15 August 2016. The deal unravelled along familiar lines: the buyer said the seller never supplied the original lease deed needed for her housing loan; the seller pointed out that on 10 May 2016 — months before the deadline — the buyer had purchased another property in Shrestha Vihar for Rs 4.25 crore without a word to him. Legal notices followed, the buyer sued for refund, and the seller counter-claimed Rs 1 crore in damages. The trial court decreed the refund with 4 per cent interest on 4 March 2021 and dismissed the counter-claim.</p>

<h2>Label versus substance</h2>
<p>The appeal turned first on characterisation. The High Court examined the agreement as a whole — the magnitude of the payment against the total price, the structure of the balance payment schedule, and the loan clause — and concluded that the Rs 1.5 crore was part payment of the sale price, not a true earnest. The consequence is decisive: part payment is refundable on failure of the transaction, whatever the document calls it, while forfeiture operates, if at all, on genuine earnest money and within the discipline of Section 74.</p>

<h2>The counter-claim and the burden of proving loss</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>The principle:</strong> quoting <em>Fateh Chand</em>, the Court reiterated that Section 74 does not justify compensation where, in consequence of the breach, no legal injury has resulted.</li>
  <li><strong>The evidence offered:</strong> the seller relied on the price of the buyer's substitute purchase elsewhere and on newspaper reports of a falling market.</li>
  <li><strong>Why it failed:</strong> a cheaper purchase of a different property proves nothing about the subject property's value, and generalised press reports are not proof of specific loss.</li>
  <li><strong>The Rule 27 application:</strong> circle rates of 2021, a 2016 sale deed and press clippings were all available at trial; none of the Order XLI Rule 27 gateways applied, so the application was dismissed.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Drafting and litigation consequences</h2>
<p>For sellers, the judgment is a checklist in reverse. If forfeiture is intended, the agreement should designate a distinct, modest earnest component separate from price instalments, state the forfeiture consequence expressly, and keep the amount within the bounds of reasonableness that courts will enforce. After a breach, the seller who wishes to retain money must assemble proof of actual loss promptly — a documented resale of the same property, or contemporaneous valuation — because the courtroom will not accept market commentary in place of evidence. For buyers, the case confirms that large signing payments in failed transactions are recoverable where the sums are in truth price instalments, and that interest, though modest at 4 per cent here, follows the decree.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> First appeals are decided on the trial record. Parties who sense a valuation battle should prove circle rates, comparable sales and expert valuations before the trial court — Order XLI Rule 27 is a narrow gate, not a second innings.</p>
</div>
